import * from os;
import * from ftps_win;

mod export ftps;

// DOCS -- public `ftps` module (UNIFIED_CORE_LIBRARY.md Phase 2). This is the
// unified, portable front of the FTPS library. It contains ZERO win32
// imports: every ws2_32 call moved into the internal Windows backend
// std/ftps_win.sr, and every entry that touches the network routes through
// the OS gate `if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS)` before calling it
// (osType() is std/os.sr's plain-Silver stand-in -- see that file for why
// the gate does not use the native os.type query).
//
// The else branch of every gate is unreachable on this backend (silverc only
// emits Windows PEs and osType() is the compile-time WINDOWS constant). For
// a future non-Windows backend the fallbacks follow the module's failure
// style: login/list/download/upload/close are silent no-ops, list() returns
// [], isLoggedIn() stays false.
//
// DISPATCH (per compiler, probed): `ftps` is builtin-reserved on NEITHER
// compiler -- silverc-rs's builtin_modules_set (src/main.rs:81 lists
// fs/json/yaml/env/url/hash/path/bom/console/math/http only) and the
// self-hosted compiler's visBuiltinModule (compiler/visibility.sr:605) plus
// its driver prepend list all exclude it. Both compilers resolve
// `import * from ftps;` as an ORDINARY SOURCE module (std/ftps.sr); no
// native dispatch exists to preserve. (The "ftps://" strings in both
// compilers' url builtins are URL-scheme checks, unrelated to this module.)
//
// PRE-SPLIT STATE (probed against the untouched std/ftps.sr before this
// rewrite): the old file NEVER compiled under either compiler. silverc-rs
// died with `Expected 'LeftParen' but found 'Dot'` and the self-hosted
// compiler with `Parse error ... expected ( but found .` -- both at the
// phantom `struct sockaddr_in addr;` / `&addr` connect call (a syntax that
// exists in no compiler), with `AF_INET`/`SOCK_STREAM` resolving to nothing
// (neither name is in either compiler's ws2_32 export table: WSAStartup
// WSACleanup socket bind listen accept recv send shutdown closesocket
// connect select setsockopt ioctlsocket htons inet_addr getaddrinfo
// freeaddrinfo WSAGetLastError only). The download/upload parameters named
// `local` are a further landmine (`local` is a reserved memory-safety
// keyword on silverc-rs). Every body was therefore dead text -- there was NO
// observable pre-split behavior to preserve beyond names, arities and
// intent.
//
// TLS STORY (honest): the pre-split TLS support was DEAD STORAGE, not a
// stub and not an implementation. The enums existed and setTLS/setCA/
// setVerifyMode wrote private fields, but NOTHING ever read them: login()
// was a bare socket+connect (no AUTH TLS, no handshake, no certificate code
// anywhere in the file), and download()/upload() were empty. This split
// preserves exactly that shape: the knobs are still stored and still inert
// (no SCHANNEL grant, no handshake invented -- a real TLS stack is a
// separate feature). Consequence: this client speaks PLAIN FTP on the
// wire. Against an FTPS-only server that demands AUTH TLS before
// authentication, login() fails gracefully at the USER step and the
// server's reply is surfaced through getLastReply(); against a classic
// FTP server it works end to end.
//
// DEAD -> LIVE (what this split made actually work, mirroring the std/ftp.sr
// split):
// - The control channel: socket + connect with WSAStartup (the pre-split
//   file never initialized winsock) and a 30 s SO_RCVTIMEO (the pre-split
//   file had no timeout and would have hung on a silent peer), the
//   sockaddr_in image packed into an int[2] qword buffer, and DLL returns
//   normalized with the two-sided `v < 0 || v > 2147483647` check (the
//   self-host zero-extends SOCKET_ERROR to 4294967295; see std/ftps_win.sr).
// - login(): pre-split it fired a single unchecked connect (not even
//   USER/PASS). It now reads and validates the greeting (220), sends USER
//   (331, or 230 = server skips PASS) and PASS (230), and records the
//   outcome in isLoggedIn()/getLastReply() (both ADDITIVE -- the pre-split
//   surface had no way to report anything; same additions as std/ftp.sr).
//   Against a non-FTP listener the login fails gracefully: the server's
//   banner (or "" after a silent disconnect) is surfaced through
//   getLastReply() and the process exits cleanly. No exceptions anywhere.
// - list(): pre-split it returned two hard-coded fake names ("file1.txt",
//   "file2.txt") without touching the network. It now opens a real PASV
//   data channel, sends LIST and returns the data-channel lines split on
//   "\r\n" (a trailing CRLF yields a trailing "" element -- the same shape
//   std/ftp.sr produces).
// - download()/upload(): pre-split empty bodies. download() now streams
//   RETR over the data channel into the local file; upload() sends STOR and
//   the file bytes over the data channel (local content is pointer on
//   silverc-rs; see std/dns.sr's note). The fs.* receiver is the
//   native/builtin fs on both compilers (std/crypto.sr and std/csv.sr use
//   the same shape).
// - Bounded reads: readReply()'s accumulation loop is additionally bounded
//   at 16 iterations and readAll() at 4096 x 64 KB (~256 MB) so no path can
//   spin forever even against a peer that never closes.
// - QUIT: close() sends QUIT and closes immediately without waiting for the
//   221, so a dead peer cannot stall close().
//
// STANDALONE (checked): the pre-split std/ftps.sr imported ONLY
// win32.ws2_32 -- it did NOT import ftp or share code with it. This split
// keeps ftps standalone: the pure protocol helpers below are copied
// verbatim from std/ftp.sr's probed helpers (and re-probed byte-identical
// for ftps on both compilers) rather than imported, avoiding the
// cross-module int[]-helper dispatch trap documented in std/ftp.sr. Do not
// "deduplicate" them across the two modules without re-proving the
// cross-module dispatch on silverc-rs.
//
// API PRESERVATION (every public name/arity of the pre-split file,
// unchanged): enum TLSMode { Explicit, Implicit } and enum VerifyMode
// { None, Strict } -- resyntaxed as `def X = enum { ... };` because a
// top-level C-style `enum X { ... }` is REJECTED by silverc-rs ("Unexpected
// token 'Enum'"; the self-host accepts both forms, probed). The observable
// values are identical either way: members auto-increment from 0
// (DOCS/212), so Explicit=0/Implicit=1 and None=0/Strict=1 on both
// compilers. client.construct(string host, int port) (the pre-split ctor
// takes the port, so unlike std/ftp.sr no additive setPort is needed);
// setTLS(TLSMode); setCA(string); setVerifyMode(VerifyMode) (all three
// stored-and-inert, the pre-split TLS shape); login(string user, string
// pass); list(string path) -> string[]; download(string remote, string
// localPath); upload(string localPath, string remote) (the pre-split
// parameter was spelled `local` -- a reserved word on silverc-rs; parameter
// names are invisible to positional callers, so the observable API is
// unchanged); close(). The private sendCmd/recvResp helper names are
// preserved; recvResp keeps its raw-bytes shape (the multi-line reply
// accumulation lives in the new private readReply, the std/ftp.sr shape).
// ADDITIVE (the std/ftp.sr precedent, documented): getLastReply() returns
// the raw text of the last control-channel reply ("" before the first read
// -- the graceful-failure surface), isLoggedIn() is true only after a fully
// accepted USER/PASS exchange. Also additive and invisible: the private
// lastReply/loggedIn fields, and construct() now initializes sock to -1 and
// caPath to "" (the pre-split construct left them uninitialized -- a NULL
// deref had close() ever run on a never-connected client).
//
// CALLER NOTES: instantiate the client with `var c = new client(...);` --
// a lowercase class arriving from a star-imported module cannot be spelled
// as a local's type on silverc-rs (per-file parsing: `client c` dies with
// "unknown keyword"; `new mod.client()` dies on BOTH compilers; `var`
// works on both, probed). Strings returned across this module's boundary
// (getLastReply) should be assigned to a local before being concatenated
// into larger expressions (silverc-rs stringifies the return pointer in
// expression position; see std/dns.sr's RS-ONLY CALLER LANDMINE). There is
// no server class -- the pre-split file never had one and none was
// invented (use std/ftp.sr's server if a plain-FTP listener is needed).

def TLSMode = enum { Explicit, Implicit };

def VerifyMode = enum { None, Strict };

public int replyCode(string resp) {
    if (resp.length < 3) { return -1; }
    int code = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        int c = resp[i] & 0xFF;
        if (!isDigit(c)) { return -1; }
        code = code * 10 + (c - 48);
    }
    return code;
}

// True when `resp` already contains a terminating FTP reply line: some
// "\r\n"-delimited line whose first three chars are digits and whose fourth
// char is a space (RFC 959's "nnn " terminal form). Multi-line replies
// ("nnn-text ... nnn text") complete only at the space form, so the
// wrapper's readers keep accumulating until this fires or the bounded
// loop exhausts.
public bool replyComplete(string resp) {
    int lineStart = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < resp.length; i++) {
        if (resp[i] == 13 && i + 1 < resp.length && resp[i + 1] == 10) {
            int lineLen = i - lineStart;
            if (lineLen >= 4) {
                if (isDigit(resp[lineStart] & 0xFF) && isDigit(resp[lineStart + 1] & 0xFF) && isDigit(resp[lineStart + 2] & 0xFF) && resp[lineStart + 3] == 32) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            i++;
            lineStart = i + 1;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Extracts the PASV host "h1.h2.h3.h4" from a "227 ... (h1,h2,h3,h4,p1,p2)"
// reply ("" on any malformed input).
public string pasvHost(string reply) {
    int[] nums = new int[6];
    int count = pasvNums(reply, nums);
    if (count != 6) { return ""; }
    return nums[0] + "." + nums[1] + "." + nums[2] + "." + nums[3];
}

// Extracts the PASV port p1*256+p2 from the same reply (-1 on malformed
// input). Deliberately *256, not a shift: shift expressions inside
// one-liner methods are unreliable on the self-hosted compiler.
public int pasvPort(string reply) {
    int[] nums = new int[6];
    int count = pasvNums(reply, nums);
    if (count != 6) { return -1; }
    return nums[4] * 256 + nums[5];
}

private bool isDigit(int c) {
    return c >= 48 && c <= 57;
}

// Scans the parenthesized section of a PASV reply for its integer tokens
// ("h1,h2,h3,h4,p1,p2"), storing the first six into `dest` and returning
// how many were found -- callers require exactly 6, anything else means
// the reply was not a well-formed PASV echo.
// NOTE: deliberately scans reply[i] by index (NO substr) and fills a
// caller-owned array (NO int[] return value). silverc-rs's two-arg
// substr(start, X) treats X as an END offset while the self-host treats it
// as a LENGTH -- identical only when start is 0 (see
// silverc-rs/src/string_ops.rs emit_string_substring), so substring
// extraction here would silently disagree across compilers. And a helper
// RETURNING an int[] mis-dispatches across a module boundary on
// silverc-rs (std/ftp.sr's probe: the count arrived 0 for valid input on
// rs while the self-host returned 6). Same-module here, the shape is the
// probed-safe one from std/ftp.sr either way.
private int pasvNums(string reply, int[] dest) {
    int open = reply.indexOf("(");
    if (open < 0) { return 0; }
    int closeIdx = reply.indexOf(")");
    if (closeIdx < 0 || closeIdx < open) { return 0; }
    int count = 0;
    int val = 0;
    bool inNum = false;
    for (int i = open + 1; i < closeIdx; i++) {
        int c = reply[i] & 0xFF;
        if (isDigit(c)) {
            val = val * 10 + (c - 48);
            inNum = true;
        } else {
            if (inNum) {
                if (count < 6) { dest[count] = val; }
                count++;
                val = 0;
                inNum = false;
            }
        }
    }
    if (inNum) {
        if (count < 6) { dest[count] = val; }
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}

class client {
    private string host;
    private int port;
    private TLSMode tlsMode;
    private string caPath;
    private VerifyMode verifyMode;
    private int sock;
    private string lastReply;
    private bool loggedIn;

    construct(string host, int port) {
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
        this.tlsMode = TLSMode.Explicit;
        this.verifyMode = VerifyMode.Strict;
        this.caPath = "";
        this.sock = -1;
        this.lastReply = "";
        this.loggedIn = false;
    }

    // Stored and NEVER read (the pre-split TLS shape, preserved verbatim):
    // no handshake consults tlsMode, so Explicit vs Implicit changes
    // nothing. Kept so the public API stays identical.
    public void setTLS(TLSMode mode) { this.tlsMode = mode; }

    // Stored and NEVER read: no certificate store exists in this module.
    public void setCA(string path) { this.caPath = path; }

    // Stored and NEVER read: no verification is performed (there is no
    // TLS layer to verify against).
    public void setVerifyMode(VerifyMode mode) { this.verifyMode = mode; }

    // ADDITIVE (the std/ftp.sr precedent): the raw text of the last server
    // reply on the control channel ("" before the first read). This is the
    // graceful-failure surface: against a non-FTP listener it holds the
    // banner the peer sent, or "" when the peer said nothing and closed.
    public string getLastReply() {
        return this.lastReply;
    }

    // ADDITIVE: true only after a fully accepted USER/PASS exchange (or a
    // 230 login-without-PASS).
    public bool isLoggedIn() {
        return this.loggedIn;
    }

    // PLAIN FTP on the wire (see the TLS STORY above): no AUTH TLS is sent.
    // An FTPS-only server that requires TLS before authentication fails the
    // USER step gracefully here.
    public void login(string user, string pass) {
        if (osType() != OSTypes.WINDOWS) { return; }
        this.sock = ftpsWinConnect(this.host, this.port);
        if (this.sock < 0) {
            this.sock = -1;
            this.lastReply = "";
            return;
        }
        this.readReply();
        if (replyCode(this.lastReply) != 220) {
            this.fail();
            return;
        }
        this.sendCmd("USER " + user);
        this.readReply();
        int code = replyCode(this.lastReply);
        if (code != 331 && code != 230) {
            this.fail();
            return;
        }
        if (code != 230) {
            this.sendCmd("PASS " + pass);
            this.readReply();
            if (replyCode(this.lastReply) != 230) {
                this.fail();
                return;
            }
        }
        this.loggedIn = true;
    }

    public string[] list(string path) {
        string[] empty = [];
        if (osType() != OSTypes.WINDOWS) { return empty; }
        if (!this.loggedIn) { return empty; }
        int data = this.openData();
        if (data < 0) { return empty; }
        this.sendCmd("LIST " + path);
        string content = this.readAll(data);
        ftpsWinClose(data);
        this.readReply();
        return content.split("\r\n");
    }

    // NOTE: the second parameter is spelled localPath, not `local` -- `local`
    // is a reserved word on silverc-rs ("Expected expression" wherever it
    // appears as an identifier), a pre-split landmine (the old signature
    // used it). Parameter names are invisible to positional callers, so the
    // observable API is unchanged.
    public void download(string remote, string localPath) {
        if (osType() != OSTypes.WINDOWS) { return; }
        if (!this.loggedIn) { return; }
        int data = this.openData();
        if (data < 0) { return; }
        this.sendCmd("RETR " + remote);
        string content = this.readAll(data);
        ftpsWinClose(data);
        this.readReply();
        fs.writeString(localPath, content);
    }

    public void upload(string localPath, string remote) {
        if (osType() != OSTypes.WINDOWS) { return; }
        if (!this.loggedIn) { return; }
        string content = fs.readString(localPath);
        int data = this.openData();
        if (data < 0) { return; }
        this.sendCmd("STOR " + remote);
        ftpsWinSend(data, content, content.length);
        ftpsWinClose(data);
        this.readReply();
    }

    public void close() {
        if (this.sock >= 0 && osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS) {
            this.sendCmd("QUIT");
            ftpsWinClose(this.sock);
        }
        this.sock = -1;
        this.loggedIn = false;
    }

    // Sends one command line (CRLF appended). No-op on a dead channel --
    // every caller pairs it with a bounded read, so the failure surfaces
    // there instead.
    private void sendCmd(string cmd) {
        if (this.sock < 0) { return; }
        ftpsWinSend(this.sock, cmd + "\r\n", cmd.length + 2);
    }

    // One bounded read of the control channel (raw bytes as a string; ""
    // on disconnect/timeout -- pre-split recvResp shape).
    private string recvResp() {
        if (this.sock < 0) { return ""; }
        return ftpsWinReceive(this.sock, 4096);
    }

    // Accumulates control-channel reads into lastReply until a terminating
    // "nnn " line is seen (replyComplete), the peer goes quiet, or 16
    // reads pass -- whichever comes first. Overwrites, never appends
    // across commands: a stale reply must not leak into the next check.
    private void readReply() {
        string acc = "";
        for (int guard = 0; guard < 16; guard++) {
            string chunk = this.recvResp();
            if (chunk.length <= 0) { break; }
            acc += chunk;
            if (replyComplete(acc)) { break; }
        }
        this.lastReply = acc;
    }

    // Opens the passive data channel for a transfer: PASV on the control
    // channel, parse host/port, connect. Returns -1 (and leaves the
    // control channel open) on any failure.
    private int openData() {
        this.sendCmd("PASV");
        this.readReply();
        string host = pasvHost(this.lastReply);
        int port = pasvPort(this.lastReply);
        if (port < 0 || host.length <= 0) { return -1; }
        int data = -1;
        if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS) {
            data = ftpsWinConnect(host, port);
        }
        return data;
    }

    // Drains a data channel until EOF/timeout (bounded at 4096 x 64 KB
    // reads, ~256 MB -- the guard makes the loop finite even against a
    // peer that never closes).
    private string readAll(int fd) {
        string acc = "";
        for (int guard = 0; guard < 4096; guard++) {
            string chunk = ftpsWinReceive(fd, 65536);
            if (chunk.length <= 0) { break; }
            acc += chunk;
        }
        return acc;
    }

    // Drops the connection and marks the client logged out; lastReply
    // keeps whatever the server last said so the caller can surface it.
    private void fail() {
        this.loggedIn = false;
        if (this.sock >= 0) {
            ftpsWinClose(this.sock);
        }
        this.sock = -1;
    }
}
